# Plan — one module per language, valid for ANY version, and made to prove it

Owner directive (2026-08-19), two messages, one design:

> *"we need to read EVERYTHING these sites contain, they generally have different versions adding and
> removing different things, if we just keep track of that PLUS what we already have then versioning to
> a specific version and recommending upgrades or linting older versions becomes trivial and basically
> no bigger"*

> *"`.any` is great, in fact all of these modules should be `.any` and the name should be removed
> because it's implied, but if it's a `.any` WE MUST VALIDATE IT REALLY CAN DO ANY VERSION OF THAT
> LANGUAGE, AND IT MUST VERIFY EVERYTHING THROUGH THE SAME CHAIN WE WORKED SO HARD TO CREATE, IT SELF
> TRAINS ITSELF BECAUSE OF THIS"*

## What is wrong today, measured

The first real registry published 80 modules. **66 of them were named `@any`** — and every one of those
was named that way because `detect_version(lang)` found no such toolchain installed on the training
machine. `css`, `vue`, `fish`, `elixir` are not "valid for any version"; they are "we did not look".
An absence of information was wearing a universality claim.

The other 14 are the mirror defect: they carry the *publisher's* exact toolchain
(`rust@1.95.0`, `python@3.12.3`), and `registry_fetch` matches that string exactly. A consumer one
patch release ahead matches nothing and crawls the whole documentation site itself — the precise waste
the registry exists to end.

So the distribution key is wrong in both directions, and the label is false in 66 of 80 cases.

## The deeper fact: the version is IN the pages, and we throw it away

The seeds are current-series or living URLs (`docs.python.org/3/reference/`,
`developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/`, `doc.rust-lang.org/reference/`). Those pages state
their own version history inline, next to the construct they describe:

- `Deprecated since version 3.11` · `Removed in version 3.13` · `Changed in version 3.12`
- MDN's per-feature compatibility and `Deprecated` / `Experimental` banners

Confirmed present in the 981MB of pages this machine has already crawled. `lint_attest` reads exactly
these notices — and captures only the *fact* of deprecation, never the version attached to it
(`attests("Deprecated since version 3.11")` → true; the `3.11` is read past and discarded).

That is the whole gap. We are already reading the sentence that carries the answer.

## The model

**A language has ONE module. Every rule inside carries the versions it holds for.**

- `since` — the first version the rule applies to (from `New in version X`, `Added in X`).
- `until` — the version it stops applying at (from `Removed in version Y`).
- Neither ⇒ the rule is version-independent, which is the common case (style, structure, most
  prohibitions).

The module file name loses its version suffix entirely — `<lang>.module.bin` — because covering every
version is no longer a claim about one file, it is a property each rule states about itself.

The match key loses the toolchain too. What determines a module's rules is WHICH DOCUMENTATION WAS
READ, and `sources_fp` already identifies that (a version-scoped doc URL puts its version inside that
fingerprint). The publisher's local compiler is provenance, not a gate.

## What this buys, per the directive

- **Lint an older version**: apply only rules whose range covers the project's version.
- **Recommend upgrades**: a rule whose `since` is ABOVE the project's version is not a violation — it
  is advice ("available from 3.12").
- **"Basically no bigger"**: correct. The rules are the same rules; each gains at most two short
  version strings. No second corpus, no per-version module.

## The validation obligation — `any` must be earned

A module may be published as version-agnostic only when **every rule in it is accounted for**, through
the same proof chain everything else goes through (`canon_plan_proven`'s fire-bad/clean-good, the
self-verify gates, PASS 31's conservation invariant). For each rule, exactly one must hold:

1. **Version-independent, proven** — the rule fires on its bad example and stays clean on its good one,
   and the documentation attached NO version statement to it.
2. **Version-scoped, proven and READ** — the rule carries a `since`/`until` taken from the docs' own
   words, and still proves fire-bad/clean-good.

Anything else is WITHHELD with a named reason and does not ship. A module with an unaccounted rule is
not published as version-agnostic — the name is a claim, and an unvalidated claim does not get made.

This is the "self trains itself" property the directive names: reading the version history gives the
machine a second axis to check itself on. A rule that says "removed in 3.13" is a claim the corpus can
be re-read against, and a rule that claims universality while sitting next to a version notice is a
contradiction the gate can catch.

## Staging

1. **Capture the version.** Where a status word the machine already recognises (deprecated / removed /
   added / changed) stands next to a version token, keep the token. Learned by adjacency, no coded
   list of version formats.
2. **Carry it on the rule** — `since` / `until` through the module codec, defaulted empty so existing
   artifacts decode unchanged.
3. **Name and key honestly** — `<lang>.module.bin`; match on `(language, train_version, sources)`.
4. **Gate the claim** — a module publishes as version-agnostic only when every rule satisfies (1) or
   (2); otherwise the offenders are named and withheld.
5. **Spend it** — consumer-side filtering by detected version, and upgrade advice for rules whose
   `since` is ahead of the project.

Steps 1–4 are what make the name honest, and must land together. Step 5 is what makes it useful, and
depends on nothing but them.
